Steady drag on smooth circular cylinders was measured in a towing tank for nominal diameters of 25.4, 38.1, and 50.8 mm. Each cylinder was tested at ten towing speeds from 0.2 to 1.1 m s\(^{-1}\), with a nominal submerged length of 0.60 m. Drag was measured with a load cell, calibrated separately for the October/November 2025 and March 2026 measurement sessions. The existing analysis calculates a mean force over the interval from 3 to 8 s and forms a drag coefficient using the projected area \(DL\). With Reynolds number provisionally calculated using the kinematic viscosity of water at 20 °C, the available plot places the measurements at approximately \(5\\times10^3\) to \(5.6\\times10^4\) and shows coefficients of order 1.1–1.2. The 1 in and 2 in series have reported mean coefficients of approximately 1.14 and 1.18, respectively; the 1.5 in series is approximately 1.16 by visual inspection of the plotted points. These results indicate broadly similar drag coefficients across the tested diameters and speeds, but interpretation is limited by the use of a single assumed viscosity, absence of documented repeat runs and uncertainty estimates, and a suspected zero drift in one 2 in run. Temperature-corrected Reynolds numbers, review of the anomalous run, and tank blockage and end-effect assessments are required before drawing quantitative conclusions.
